# React Event Objects

So far we have responded to the fact that an event happened. Often we also want details about it: not just that a button was clicked, but where the click landed; not just that a key was pressed, but which key. React passes an **event object** to every event handler that carries this information.

The event object is the first argument to your handler. You name a parameter for it (commonly `event` or `e`) and read properties off of it.

```jsx
function handleClick(event) {
  console.log(event.target);      // the element that was clicked
  console.log(event.clientX);     // horizontal position of the click
}

<button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>
```

## Synthetic Events

The object React hands you is a **synthetic event**. It is a lightweight wrapper around the browser's native DOM event, giving you a consistent set of properties and methods across browsers. It carries the most useful pieces of the native event, including `target`, along with methods like `preventDefault()` and `stopPropagation()` that we have used before.

When you do need the underlying browser event, it is available at `event.nativeEvent`.

```jsx
function handleClick(event) {
  console.log(event);              // the React SyntheticEvent
  console.log(event.nativeEvent);  // the original DOM event it wraps
}
```

Most of the time the synthetic event has everything you need, so reaching for `event.nativeEvent` is rare.

## Input Change Events

For an `onChange` handler on an input, `event.target` is the input element and `event.target.value` is the text currently in it. That is how we keep a piece of state in sync with what the user types.

```{react}
function NamePreview() {
  const [name, setName] = React.useState('');

  function handleChange(event) {
    setName(event.target.value);
  }

  return (
    <section>
      <input aria-label="Name" value={name} onChange={handleChange} />
      <p>{name}</p>
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<NamePreview />);
```

- `event.target` is the element that fired the event (the input).
- `event.target.value` is the current text in that input.

## Keyboard Events

Different event types carry different properties. A keyboard event exposes `event.key`, the value of the key that was pressed, while a mouse event exposes things like `event.clientX` and `event.clientY`. Here we use an `onKeyDown` handler to read which key was pressed, and when the user presses Enter we read the full value from `event.target.value`.

```{react}
function KeyLogger() {
  const [message, setMessage] = React.useState('Press a key in the box.');

  function handleKeyDown(event) {
    if (event.key === 'Enter') {
      setMessage('You submitted: ' + event.target.value);
    } else {
      setMessage('You pressed: ' + event.key);
    }
  }

  return (
    <section>
      <input aria-label="Keys" onKeyDown={handleKeyDown} />
      <p>{message}</p>
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<KeyLogger />);
```

- `event.key` is the pressed key as a string, for example `'a'`, `'Enter'`, or `'ArrowUp'`.
- Because event types differ, a keyboard handler has `event.key` while a mouse handler does not. Read the properties that match the event you are handling.

## Preventing Default Behavior

For form submissions, you often call `event.preventDefault()` so the browser does not reload the page and React can handle the submit itself.

```jsx
function handleSubmit(event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  // handle the form data here
}
```

## Exercises

1. Which property of a keyboard event tells you which key was pressed?

   ```{mcq}
   Which property of a keyboard event tells you which key was pressed?
   correct: Correct. A keyboard event exposes the pressed key as `event.key`.
   wrong: Recall that keyboard events and mouse events carry different properties.
   [x] `event.key`
   [ ] `event.value`
   [ ] `event.clientX`
   [ ] `event.pressed`
   ```

2. Finish `handleChange` so the preview updates as the user types.

   ```{react}
   function Preview() {
     const [text, setText] = React.useState('');

     function handleChange(event) {
       setText('');
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <input aria-label="Preview text" value={text} onChange={handleChange} />
         <p>{text}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Preview />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Preview() {
     const [text, setText] = React.useState('');

     function handleChange(event) {
       setText(event.target.value);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <input aria-label="Preview text" value={text} onChange={handleChange} />
         <p>{text}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Preview />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('input').should('exist').run('The input should render', 'The input renders');
     // lite-cy's type() assigns the input's value directly, which React's value
     // tracker ignores for a controlled input, so the handler is checked by shape.
     wrap(/setText\s*\(\s*event\.target\.value\s*\)/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Read the typed text from `event.target.value` and pass it to `setText`', 'Read the value from the event');
     wrap(/setText\s*\(\s*['\"]{2}\s*\)/.test(source)).should('equal', false).run('`setText(\'\')` throws the typed text away; pass the event\'s value instead', 'Stopped clearing the text');
   }, 50);
   ```

3. React passes a synthetic event to your handler. How do you reach the original browser event it wraps?

   ```{mcq}
   React passes a synthetic event to your handler. How do you reach the original browser event it wraps?
   correct: Correct. The native DOM event is available at `event.nativeEvent`.
   wrong: The React event wraps the native one and exposes it on a property.
   [x] `event.nativeEvent`
   [ ] `event.original`
   [ ] `event.dom`
   [ ] `event.rawEvent`
   ```

